     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet. The total daily dose is far above the safe maximum, so I need you to confirm what you meant.\n\n**The problem:** 1700 mg three times a day comes to **5100 mg/day**. The maximum for metformin is:\n- **2550 mg/day** for immediate-release (for example, 850 mg three times a day)\n- **2000 mg/day** for extended-release\n\nSo the order as written is about twice the immediate-release maximum. That raises the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI side effects, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n**It may have been meant as one of these:**\n1. **Metformin 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals** (2550 mg/day, the immediate-release maximum). 1700 mg might be the total for two doses, or 850 might have been doubled by mistake.\n2. **Metformin 1700 mg total per day**, given as 850 mg twice daily with meals.\n3. **Something else**, such as a lower starting dose and titration plan if the patient is new to metformin. A typical start is 500 mg once or twice daily, going up weekly as tolerated.\n\nWhich dose and frequency should I enter? Please also say whether it's immediate- or extended-release. If you really do intend a dose above the labeled maximum, tell me the clinical reason and I'll add it to the order note so pharmacy can review it."
      }
     ],
